<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wonhyo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">617–686</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wonhyo was a Buddhist monk and scholar of Silla, one of the most influential thinkers in Korean history. Setting out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, he is said to have turned back after realising that all things arise from the mind. He wrote many commentaries seeking to reconcile different Buddhist teachings, spread Pure Land devotion among ordinary people and was the father of the scholar Seol Chong.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wonhyo.jpg" alt="Wonhyo" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kernbeisser (a flickr user) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Joseon_Dynasty-Gilt-bronze_seated_Avalokitesvara_at_Beopjusa_Temple-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
